  Item_78874_Name = {
    Text = "Thirsting Brush"
  },
  Item_78874_StoryDesc = {
    Text = "On the seventh day of his transformation into a ghoul, he moans in agony as his limbs gradually rot.\n\nEvery slight movement tugs at his sensitive nerves, erupting in tearing pain...\nmixed with horrifying sparks of inspiration.\n\nYet, the perplexing shadows always loom over this artist—what kind of brush can depict the most extreme reality?\n\n\nPickman's weak eyes scan the area, from the stones by the mansion to the branches above the graves, and finally, his gaze locks onto the exposed white bone in his shriveled, rotting right leg.\n\nAs if hearing a sacred summons, Pickman extracts the leg bone, repeatedly rubbing away the adhered flesh and blood, turning it into a hard brush.\n\nHe sharpens the tip into a menacing fang, but before it can bite into the canvas, it first pierces the owner's bony fingertip.\n\nPickman, in pain, releases his grip, and the brush escapes his control.\n\nIt dances! It leaps! It twirls!\n\nIt wildly sweeps across the canvas, carving chaotic lines of madness.\n\nThe lines proliferate and grow wildly, dragging out a constantly expanding, strangely shaped dark smear that slowly but decisively gnaws at Pickman's throat.\n\nA momentary ecstasy pierces his remaining heart like a sharp blade—the swollen organ, in its chaotic form, fills him with tremors of fear, yet revives his waning senses.\n\nThey directly and sharply signify pain, strangeness, and desire in a disordered manner, erupting with unabashed, shocking power.\n\nIn this inversion, Pickman seems to draw closer to the sacred realm of 'beauty'.\n\n\nOnce everything has calmed down, Pickman extends his clawed hand, now corroded, repeatedly stroking the pale brush.\n\nFrom this still humanoid shell, a more intense and urgent desire spews forth.\n\nHe will together with this brush, complete the final stroke of that monumental painting."
  },
  Item_78877_Desc = {
    Text = "Wheel of Destiny with the Realm Mastery attribute."
  },
  Item_78877_Name = {
    Text = "Rota Fortunae"
  },
  Item_78877_StoryDesc = {
    Text = [[
On the day of her seventh birthday, her father rested a spinning wheel called "Moirae" upon her lap.

It bore the name of three Goddesses of Fate long forsaken by prayer, yet the wheel itself was sleek and new, its copper axle gleaming with a metallic sheen, cold to the touch and heavy with silence.

From that day forth, Arachne took up the wheel of fate-not as a choice, but as a sentence. For her unintended words always came true in time, and she could not turn away from the weight of such a burden.

Day after day, she wove without respite, watching the threads of fate coil silently around the intricate wheel, weaving for the world the tremor of a first kiss, the neighing of warhorses, the first cry of a newborn, and the jagged epitaphs carved into stone.

Among thousands of tangled filaments, she selected the "better" paths. She beckoned the dark tide of plague to ebb; she spared history from the consuming flames of war; she allowed civilizations to rebuild from the ruins...

Yet, to sustain that web stretching across time and space, she also wove the strands leading to madness, betrayal, or eternal night.

"What is truly better?"

The silent wheel offered no answer. It only continued to spin.

Virtue and vice, cradles and coffins-all flickered alternately along the same thread.

Young as she was then, she did not yet know the shape of her own fate, yet at the end of countless threads, she had already glimpsed the world's end.

"If the world cannot escape its ruin..."

"No. I love this world."

Arachne gazed upon the wheel, letting the unspoken thought dissolve into silence.

"Then... what of my own fate?"

She murmured to herself, with only hollow echoes wandering between the four walls.

Countless civilizations rose and crumbled upon the web of fate. Pyramids slumbered beneath the sand; spacecraft streaked across seas of stars; languages changed like the turning of seasons; and the names of gods were interred in the dust of forgotten lexicons...

And she sat there always, turning the wheel in her hands, watching humanity reenact its cycle of hope and devastation.

Until, at last, a gentle voice that transcended the eternal prison pierced the silence of the ages.

"Sister..."

She finally withdrew her gaze from the apocalypse, looking down at Nefraea beside her. In those eyes, there was no judgment, no fear, only complete trust, like a blank sheet of paper awaiting the first stroke of ink.

In that moment, Arachne finally understood: love and duty, hatred and ruin-all were the same in the eyes of fate.

And so she gathered those memories she had once snipped and discarded-the pain, the regret, the loss that should have come but never did-and braided them into a single cord. Then, as the heaviest yet most tender of gifts, she tied it around Nefraea's wrist.

"Our bond?" Arachne smiled. "It is fate."

Arachne no longer needed to see her own fate clearly.

For in the moment she picked up the wheel at age seven, she had already chosen the direction of her future.

And the wheel of fate would go on turning. It would bear witness to the battle she was about to enter-a battle with no final curtain call.]]
  },
  Item_78878_Desc = {
    Text = "Wheel of Destiny with the Death Resistance attribute."
  },
  Item_78878_Name = {
    Text = "Navigator at Sea"
  },
  Item_78878_StoryDesc = {
    Text = "Toot—Toot—\n\nAs the sound of the horn echoed, the cruise ship safely docked under the guidance of Corposant.\n\nPassengers laughed as they descended the gangway, tipping their hats to thank her, while sailors rushed over to express their praise.\n\n\"Well done, Corposant. If it weren't for your early warning, we would have been swallowed by the storm.\"\n\n\"Yeah, thanks to your correct guidance, we didn't get lost in the sea!\"\n\nThe sailors laughed, patted her shoulder, and gave her a thumbs-up.\n\nIn fact, Corposant could never adapt to such close social interactions; her rigid and rational mind had no thoughts beyond \"work\" and \"duty.\"\n\nSo, she could only attribute this to a part of \"work\" and \"duty.\"\n\n\"I was just doing my job. As a helmsman, my duty is to guide you safely home.\"\n\nA polite but dull response, yet the sailors who knew her well laughed heartily, bidding her farewell, singing sea shanties, and leaving the harbor with arms over each other.\n\n\"The Nautilus can finally set sail. Depart, Nautilus.\"\n\nThe singing gradually faded away, while Corposant remained on the shore, preparing for the future voyage.\n\nThe sea is unpredictable, no helmsman can guarantee victory every time; she cannot either. But she is willing to spend countless hours to increase even the slightest odds of success.\n\nChecking, patrolling, learning, verifying, it seems that \"work\" is the only thing left in her life.\n\nYet she is willing because all of this is for that moment, the successful helmsmanship, leading everyone home safely."
  },
