  Item_96640_Desc = {
    Text = "Mason recently received a paper umbrella. The umbrella was a marvel of bamboo and tension, a gift from a Mythag Investigator returning from the East. Its bright red canopy was deceptively fragile; Mason had feared the rain would pierce the paper, yet it held firm. Standing beneath that hazy crimson glow, enveloped in the scent of fragrance oil and rain, she felt a rare moment of stillness—a reprieve from the noise in her mind.\nWhen the invitation to the Mythag autumn banquet arrived, Mason knew she had to honor the umbrella. She had once been the finest textile worker in Londinium, and though Eastern patterns were a mystery to her, she let the library's sparse descriptions guide her needle.\nUnder the dim hum of her desk lamp, the dress took shape: white gauze, delicate lace, and long, sweeping sleeves that mimicked the silhouettes of the East. She added a splash of crimson to the hem and sleeve linings to mirror the umbrella's vibrance.\n\"There,\" Mason whispered, smoothing the fabric on the mannequin. \"Now, it belongs.\"\nShe reached out to touch the lace, but her hand froze before snapping up to cradle her head.\n\"You think you're worthy of something so pure, you filthy fool?\" The persecutor, a jagged identity forged from Mason's deepest self-loathing, surged toward the front. Her limbs writhed in pain as they tangled and tore into a mess—screaming, rolling, and blood splattering everywhere.\n\"Hahaha, not enough! Not red enough! It needs to be as red as that umbrella!\"\nWhen the amnesia finally lifted minutes—or perhaps hours—later, Mason was on the floor. Her limbs ached with the phantom heat of a struggle she couldn't remember. Her vision cleared to a scene of horror: the cuffs and hem of the white gauze were no longer pure. They were wet, stained with the frantic, jagged splatters of her own blood.\nMason scrambled to the washbasin, her hands trembling violently. She scrubbed until her skin was raw, but the blood didn't disappear; it only blossomed into stubborn, pale-rose patches across the delicate fabric.\n\"But... the banquet is tomorrow...\" She sank to the floor, weeping for the \"useless\" girl who couldn't even keep her own hands from betraying her.\n\"Sister? Is it ready?\"\nThe door creaked open. Jenkin burst in, pulling a shy Aigis by the hand. \"Aigis wants to learn how to—\"\nThe children stopped. They saw the damp, stained dress and Mason hurriedly wiping her eyes, trying to feign a hollow smile.\n\"It's... it's nothing,\" Mason lied, her voice thin. \"I had an accident. I'll just wear something else. This one... I'll save it for next time.\"\nAigis, looking even sadder than Mason, stroked the carefully sewn lace. Suddenly flushed, she anxiously tugged at Mason's sleeve.\n\"On the way here,\" Aigis whispered, her voice brightening with a sudden spark, \"Jenkin and I saw a painter in the grove. He was painting the maple leaves as they fell.\" She looked up at Mason. \"What if we ask him to paint over the stains? This red... it's already the color of the forest. It could be maple leaves!\"\nJenkin let out a shout of \"Genius!\" that echoed through the small room.\nThe knot in Mason's chest loosened. She pulled the two children into a fierce embrace, her tears finally turning from grief to gratitude.\nThe next evening, beneath a sky of falling embers, Mason walked the campus path. She held the red umbrella high, and as the wind stirred her white skirt, the painted maple leaves seemed to dance."
  },
