  Item_97991_Desc = {
    Text = [[
A truly good holiday should be an excuse for a wild celebration. To be truly pleasing, its origin is particularly important. A holiday that is too minor, like one for planting trees or commemorating a foolish groundhog, doesn't offer enough reason for indulgence. On the other hand, a holiday that is too grand, like the solemn Silverfrost Eve, becomes overly serious and bound by too many rules.
Of all the days on the calendar, the one Murphy looks forward to most is All-Hallows Eve. This wonderfully thoughtful holiday naturally features a nighttime party where everyone dresses up in bizarre costumes. Best of all, on this day, countless desserts and candies appear in every corner of Mythag.
Fruit gummies, liquor-filled chocolates, milk candies, mints, toffees... Ha! Wasn't this holiday created just for ladies like her?
Murphy eagerly put on her pointed witch hat, changed into a fluffy pumpkin dress, and carefully selected the largest pumpkin basket before heading out.
But her excitement was short-lived. "What? You say these candies are only for children?" she asked, watching Jenkin walk away with a full haul. She raised her eyebrows in disbelief, her tentacles flicking with dissatisfaction under her skirt.
Grinding her teeth, she let out a cold laugh and slammed her basket onto the counter. "If I can't take them, then fine! I'm not a child anymore; I'm a robber! This is a robbery! I'll count to three, and you will pack all your candies for me. Hurry up!"
The flustered restaurant waiter had no choice but to clear the candy table. He could only watch as the lady swaggered away with her overflowing basket, laughing wildly.
That strange laughter echoed across the campus all night. Soon after, a rumor began to spread through Mythag: on the night of All-Hallows Eve, anyone carrying candy would be found by a ghostly, candy-hunting witch. The only way to escape her clutches was to faithfully surrender every last sweet.]]
  },
